Kotlin <code>build.gradle.kts</code>Groovy <code>build.gradle</code> <pre><code>plugins {
    id("xyz.wagyourtail.unimined") version "..."
    id("xyz.wagyourtail.unimined.adapter-forgix") version "..."
}

unimined {
    minecraft {
        version = "1.20.1"
        loader("fabric", "0.16.0")

        sourceSet("forge") {
            version = "1.20.1"
            loader("forge", "47.2.0")
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>plugins {
    id 'xyz.wagyourtail.unimined' version '...'
    id 'xyz.wagyourtail.unimined.adapter-forgix' version '...'
}

unimined {
    minecraft {
        version.set("1.20.1")
        loader("fabric", "0.16.0")

        sourceSet("forge") {
            version.set("1.20.1")
            loader("forge", "47.2.0")
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>That's the whole configuration — <code>mergeLoaderJars</code> discovers every bound non-auxiliary environment's production jar automatically. A typical multi-loader project writes zero merge config.</p> <p>Forgix's own Gradle plugin is not required — its <code>merge(name)</code> DSL and <code>mergeJars</code> task only support multi-project layouts (merge names must be subproject names, and its jar-resolution heuristic checks the project before <code>inputJar</code>), so flat env-per-source-set projects can't use it. The adapter drives the layout-agnostic engine directly from <code>mergeLoaderJars</code> instead.</p>

Kotlin <code>build.gradle.kts</code>Groovy <code>build.gradle</code> <pre><code>plugins {
    id("xyz.wagyourtail.unimined") version "..."
    id("xyz.wagyourtail.unimined.jvmdg") version "..."
}

unimined {
    minecraft {
        version = "1.7.10"
        loader("forge", "10.13.4.1614")
        // global defaults — opts every env in:
        // compile at 21, emit each env's era floor
        jvmdowngrader {
            source.set(21)
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>plugins {
    id 'xyz.wagyourtail.unimined' version '...'
    id 'xyz.wagyourtail.unimined.jvmdg' version '...'
}

unimined {
    minecraft {
        version.set("1.7.10")
        loader("forge", "10.13.4.1614")
        // global defaults — opts every env in:
        // compile at 21, emit each env's era floor
        jvmdowngrader {
            source.set(21)
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Applying the plugin is not the opt-in — it only adds a <code>jvmdowngrader</code> extension to every environment and a global one on <code>minecraft</code> itself, with <code>enabled</code> defaulting to <code>false</code> everywhere. Envs opt in by configuring either spec: <code>jvmdowngrader { }</code>, <code>jvmdowngrader(N)</code>, or <code>enabled.set(true)</code> — and <code>enabled.set(false)</code> inside a block still wins, so the DSL can't re-enable an env you meant to keep off.</p> <p><code>minecraft { jvmdowngrader { } }</code> is the global spec — its values apply to every environment in the extension. Per-env specs on <code>sourceSet("x") { jvmdowngrader { } }</code> override it, and for any flag the precedence is: the env's own set, then its inherited envs (nearest first), then the global spec, then the built-in default. An env can opt back out under a global enable with <code>enabled.set(false)</code>.</p>

